   105	## 2. Recursive signed delta-sequence-to-braid compiler
   106	
   107	For a reduced delta sequence `R=(r0,...,rh)`, let
   108	
   109	```text
   110	w = gcd(r0,...,r_(h-1)).                               (2.1)
   111	```
   112	
   113	If `h=1`, the compiler uses the `r1`-strand torus braid
   114	
   115	```text
   116	(sigma1 ... sigma_(r1-1))^r0,                          (2.2)
   117	```
   118	
   119	whose closure is `T(r1,r0)`.  If `h>1`, the normalized prefix
   120	
   121	```text
   122	R'=(r0/w,...,r_(h-1)/w)                                (2.3)
   123	```
   124	
   125	is again a reduced delta sequence, and the standard Puiseux/cabling
   126	dictionary gives
   127	
   128	```text
   129	K(R) = C_(w,rh)(K(R')).                                (2.4)
   130	```
   131	
   132	The compiler realizes (2.4) for an arbitrary signed companion braid, not only
   133	the winding-two/two-braid family.  Let `beta` be an `s`-strand companion word,
   134	let `e(beta)` be its exponent sum, and let `Cab_w(beta)` replace each
   135	`sigma_i` by the positive permutation braid that interchanges the adjacent
   136	blocks of `w` parallel strands.  Put
   137	
   138	```text
   139	delta_w = sigma1 ... sigma_(w-1)
   140	Beta_(w,q) = Cab_w(beta) delta_w^(q-w e(beta)).          (2.5)
   141	```
   142	
   143	Here `delta_w` acts on the first parallel block.  Blackboard parallelization
   144	contributes `w e(beta)` to the pattern meridian, so the final exponent in
   145	(2.5) is exactly the zero-framing correction.  The closure of (2.5) is the
   146	zero-framed cable `C_(w,q)(closure(beta))`.  Negative companion crossings use
   147	the inverse block switch, and negative `q` uses the signed inverse power.
   148	
   149	Induction in (2.2)--(2.5) gives a braid on exactly `r1` strands for every
   150	delta sequence, including the deep rows and the 18-strand rows at conductor
   151	26.  The compiler checks that every signed word closes to one component.
