   130	
   131	## 2. Exact nearby-fibre genus for arbitrary affine singularities
   132	
   133	Choose a reduced equation
   134	
   135	```text
   136	B=f^(-1)(0).                                              (2.1)
   137	```
   138	
   139	The normalization `A1` has one end, so `B` has one normalization place at
   140	infinity and its link `K_infinity` is a knot.  Corrected Neumann--Rudolph
   141	Lemma 7.1 makes `f` good.  Choose a sufficiently small nonzero regular value
   142	`epsilon`, with no other critical value between `0` and `epsilon`.  Goodness
   143	prevents vanishing cycles at infinity, so `f^(-1)(epsilon)` is obtained from
   144	`B` by smoothing precisely its affine singularities and has the same infinity
   145	knot.
   146	
   147	Let `r_p` be the number of local branches at `p`, and let `M_p` be its local
   148	Milnor fibre.  A compact core of the normalization is a disk.  Remove one
   149	small disk around each of the `r_p` normalization preimages of every `p`, then
   150	glue in `M_p`.  Gluing along circles changes no Euler characteristic, and
   151	
   152	```text
   153	chi(M_p)=1-mu_p.
   154	```
   155	
   156	Consequently the compact nearby fibre `F_epsilon` has
   157	
   158	```text
   159	chi(F_epsilon)
   160	 = 1-sum_p r_p + sum_p(1-mu_p)
   161	 = 1-sum_p(mu_p+r_p-1)
   162	 = 1-2 sum_p delta_p.                                    (2.2)
   163	```
   164	
   165	Its only boundary component is `K_infinity`.  Therefore
   166	
   167	```text
   168	g(F_epsilon)=Delta_aff(B).                                (2.3)
   169	```
   170	
   171	Neumann's Theorem 1 makes this compact core the minimal Seifert surface, so
   172	
   173	```text
   174	g_3(K_infinity)=Delta_aff(B).                             (2.4)
   175	```
   176	
   177	Equation (2.3) is the exact generalization of the node computation.  For an
   178	ordinary node, `r=2`, `mu=1`, and `delta=1`; for a unibranch singularity the
   179	same formula detects delta even though there is no two-point normalization
   180	fibre.
   181	
   182	## 3. One place forces an iterated cable, not a connected sum
   183	
   184	The coordinate functions on the normalization are regular functions on `A1`.
   185	Thus the normalization map is a polynomial parametrization
   186	
   187	```text
   188	nu(t)=(p(t),q(t)).                                        (3.1)
   189	```
   190	
   191	All affine failures of injectivity lie in a compact set.  On the unique tail
   192	at `t=infinity`, the successive characteristic terms of `(p,q)` give the
   193	standard Puiseux/epicycle cabling construction.  Hence a sufficiently large
   194	bidisk boundary meets `B` in an iterated cable of the unknot.  This is the
   195	direct Rudolph interface.
   196	
   197	The same conclusion follows intrinsically from Neumann's rooted construction.
   198	The projective normalization is `P1` and `P1-A1` is one point, so the closure
   199	of `B` has one point at infinity.  By Neumann Theorem 2(i), the root has
   200	valency one.  The construction therefore starts with the one-component Hopf
   201	link, namely the unknot.  Since the final link also has one component, no
   202	component-adding cabling can occur; every nontrivial step replaces the current
   203	knot by a cable.
   204	
   205	Delete winding-one steps and steps that produce an unknot.  A nontrivial torus
   206	knot is prime, and a nontrivial cable of a knot is prime by Schubert.  Induction
   207	gives
   208	
   209	```text
   210	K_infinity is the unknot or a prime iterated cable.        (3.2)
   211	```
   212	
   213	This is stronger than merely saying that `K_infinity` is a graph knot.  In
   214	particular, a connected sum of two nontrivial knots cannot occur in the present
   215	one-place setup.
   216	
   217	Finally, the infinity-knot group maps onto the affine complement group while
   218	preserving generic meridians.  For a generic finite projection, let
   219	`x_1,...,x_N` be fibre meridians and `beta_1,...,beta_s` the based finite braid
   220	monodromies.  Zariski--van Kampen imposes every individual relation
   221	
   222	```text
   223	x_j=beta_v(x_j),
   224	```
   225	
   226	whereas the Artin presentation of the closed boundary braid imposes only the
   227	relations for the total braid `beta_infinity=beta_1...beta_s`.  The individual
   228	relations imply the total ones, giving a meridian-preserving surjection
   229	
   230	```text
   231	pi1(S3-K_infinity) ->> pi1(A2-B).                         (3.3)
   232	```
   233	
   234	Composing (3.3) with (0.3) gives the same transitive transposition image.
   235	Therefore it suffices below to obstruct such an image for the infinity knot.
   236	
   237	## 4. Complete genus-two graph-knot census
   238	
   239	It is useful first to classify the larger graph-knot category, before applying
   240	(3.2).  A graph knot is built from the unknot using connected sum and cabling.
   241	Schubert's formulas, with `p>=2` after trivial steps are removed, are
   242	
   243	```text
   244	g(J#L)=g(J)+g(L),
   245	g(C_(p,q)(J))=p g(J)+(p-1)(|q|-1)/2.                     (4.1)
   246	```
   247	
   248	At genus one, the only graph knots are `T(2,+/-3)`, the two trefoils.  At
   249	genus two, (4.1) gives exactly the following unoriented families, allowing all
   250	mirrors.
   251	
   252	```text
   253	(A) T(2,+/-5);
   254	(B) T(2,+/-3) # T(2,+/-3), with either chirality per factor;
   255	(C) C_(2,+/-1)(T(2,+/-3)), with either companion chirality. (4.2)
   256	```
   257	
   258	Indeed, a composite genus-two graph knot is a sum of two genus-one graph knots.
   259	For a prime cable with unknot companion, the equation
   260	
   261	```text
   262	(p-1)(|q|-1)=4
   263	```
   264	
   265	and coprimality leave only `T(2,5)` up to exchanging the torus coordinates and
   266	mirroring.  For a nontrivial companion, `p g(J)<=2` forces
   267	`p=2`, `g(J)=1`, and then `|q|=1`.
   268	
   269	The list deliberately allows more signs than complex orientation may permit;
   270	enlarging the list makes the exclusion stronger.  By (3.2), family (B) is
   271	already impossible for a one-place polynomial curve.  Families (A) and (C)
   272	are the only one-place genus-two candidates.
   273	
   274	## 5. The double-branched-cover determinant obstruction
   275	
   276	The following elementary lemma exactly tests the remaining candidates.
   277	
   278	**Lemma.**  If a knot group has a transitive representation to `S4` sending a
   279	meridian to a transposition, then its determinant is divisible by three.
   280	
   281	To prove it, note first that an image generated by transpositions is the full
   282	`S4` whenever it acts transitively on four letters.  The sign of the image is
   283	the unique map
   284	
   285	```text
   286	pi1(S3-K) -> Z/2
   287	```
   288	
   289	sending a meridian to `1`, namely mod-two abelianization.  The restriction of
   290	the `S4` quotient to its kernel surjects onto `A4`.  Killing the square of a
   291	meridian turns this kernel into the fundamental group of the double cover of
   292	`S3` branched over `K`; the square already maps to the identity.  Hence
   293	
   294	```text
   295	pi1(Sigma_2(K)) ->> A4.                                  (5.1)
   296	```
   297	
   298	Abelianizing (5.1), and using `A4_ab=Z/3`, gives
   299	
   300	```text
   301	H1(Sigma_2(K)) ->> Z/3.
   302	```
   303	
   304	Since `|H1(Sigma_2(K))|=det(K)=|Delta_K(-1)|`, the lemma follows.
   305	
   306	Now
   307	
   308	```text
   309	det(T(2,5))=5.                                           (5.2)
   310	```
   311	
   312	For a cable, the Alexander formula is, up to a Laurent unit,
   313	
   314	```text
   315	Delta_C(p,q)(J)(t)=Delta_T(p,q)(t) Delta_J(t^p).         (5.3)
   316	```
   317	
   318	The `(2,+/-1)` torus pattern is an unknot.  With the standard normalization
   319	`Delta_J(1)=1`, evaluation of (5.3) at `t=-1` gives
   320	
   321	```text
   322	det(C_(2,+/-1)(J))=|Delta_J(1)|=1.                       (5.4)
   323	```
   324	
   325	Equations (5.2)--(5.4) exclude both prime genus-two families.  Laurent-unit
   326	normalization can change `Delta(-1)` only by sign, so it cannot affect the
   327	determinant conclusion.
   328	
   329	For completeness, the genus-zero infinity knot is the unknot and cannot have
   330	a transitive transposition image on four letters.  The genus-one infinity knot
   331	is a trefoil or mirror; its two-meridian braid presentation shows that any
   332	transposition image moves at most three letters.  Together with the genus-two
   333	argument, this proves (0.4).
   334	
   335	## 6. Composite control and the ordinary-node firewall
   336	
   337	The composite family in (4.2) is a genuine group-level survivor of the
   338	determinant filter.  The connected sum of two trefoils, with either chirality,
   339	has a meridian presentation
   340	
   341	```text
   342	G=<m,a,b | mam=ama, mbm=bmb>.                            (6.1)
   343	```
   344	
   345	The assignment
   346	
   347	```text
   348	m=(23),  a=(12),  b=(34)                                 (6.2)
   349	```
   350	
   351	satisfies both braid relations and generates `S4`.  Mirrors do not change this
   352	transposition control because transpositions are their own inverses.  Thus the
   353	determinant condition is sharp within all genus-two graph knots.
   354	
   355	One must not claim that a bare ordinary-node relation automatically kills
   356	(6.2).  In fact `a=(12)` and `b=(34)` are disjoint and commute, exactly the
   357	local transposition pattern permitted by a `(2,2)` node.  To use node relators
   358	against a hypothetical composite infinity group, one would need the actual
   359	based identification of both local branch meridians; the abstract connected-
   360	sum presentation does not provide it.  The present proof makes no such
   361	unsupported identification.  It excludes (6.1) earlier and cleanly by the
   362	one-place prime-knot theorem (3.2).
   363	
   364	## 7. Exact delta-three primitive escape
   365	
   366	The total-delta threshold is sharp for the group-theoretic obstruction.  Let
   367	
   368	```text
   369	B_3={(x,y)=(t^3,t^4)}={y^3=x^4}.                         (7.1)
   370	```
   371	
   372	Its normalization is `A1`; it has one place at infinity and one unibranch
   373	affine singularity.  The semigroup `<3,4>` has gaps `{1,2,5}`, so
   374	
   375	```text
   376	Delta_aff(B_3)=3.                                        (7.2)
   377	```
   378	
   379	Its infinity knot is `T(3,4)`, the closure of the three-braid
   380	
   381	```text
   382	beta=(sigma_1 sigma_2)^4.                                (7.3)
   383	```
   384	
   385	Use the Artin action convention
   386	
   387	```text
   388	sigma_i:(A,B) |-> (A B A^(-1),A).
   389	```
   390	
   391	Starting from the fibre-meridian colors `((12),(13),(14))`, the eight letters
   392	of (7.3) give
   393	
   394	```text
   395	((12),(13),(14))
   396	((23),(12),(14))
   397	((23),(24),(12))
   398	((34),(23),(12))
   399	((34),(13),(23))
   400	((14),(34),(23))
   401	((14),(24),(34))
   402	((12),(14),(34))
   403	((12),(13),(14)).                                       (7.4)
   404	```
   405	
   406	Thus the coloring is fixed by the closed-braid relations.  The three star
   407	transpositions generate `S4`, so (7.4) is a transitive meridian-transposition
   408	quotient.  Weighted radial contraction identifies the complement of (7.1)
   409	with the `T(3,4)` knot-complement group, so this is also an affine-complement
   410	quotient, not merely a boundary coloring.
   411	
   412	The escape is an explicit unibranch `delta=3` horn.  Other total-delta-three
   413	horns, including order-three tangency of two smooth branches, remain open;
   414	the theorem does not assert that each such local type realizes an `S4`
   415	quotient.  The `T(2,7)` unibranch type still fails the determinant test, whereas
   416	the `<3,4>` type demonstrates that total delta alone cannot do more.
   417	
   418	## 8. Campaign consequence and scope
   419	
   420	The theorem closes every irreducible `A1`-normalization, one-place, connected
   421	simple quartic lane whose complete affine singularity census has total delta
   422	at most two.  In particular it closes, simultaneously:
   423	
   424	* a sole order-two tangency of two smooth branches (`A3`, delta two);
   425	* a sole unibranch `<2,5>` singularity (topological `A4`, delta two);
   426	* any two delta-one singularities, including arbitrary node/cusp combinations;
   427	* the predecessor's sole ordinary-node case.
   428	
   429	No assumption about a chosen ruling, Hurwitz packet, quasipositive
   430	factorization, or local `(2,2)` labeling is needed after the singularity delta
   431	census is known.
   432	
   433	The following are outside the conclusion.
   434	
   435	1. Total affine delta at least three.  Section 7 proves this is an essential
   436	   boundary, not an artifact of the proof.
   437	2. Reducible curves or normalization forests with more than one component.
   438	   Their root and smoothing Euler ledgers differ, and composite infinity knots
   439	   need not be excluded by (3.2).
   440	3. More than one normalization place at infinity.
   441	4. Non-transposition generic inertia, disconnected covers, or covers of degree
   442	   other than four.  The determinant lemma specifically uses
   443	   `A4_ab=Z/3`.
   444	5. Existence of a finite algebraic cover, a polynomial map, or a Keller map
   445	   realizing the delta-three control.
   446	
